Compressing server-sent events flushed one at a time
=====================================================

2026-11-15.

This study was done on top of the statistics skip and automatic tree series
(up to "experiments: add the statistics skip and automatic tree studies").
Its outcome, the SLZ_CT_EVENT content type, was then integrated on top of the
same code, see section 20. The files here:
- sse-response.txt: a real capture of an SSE response.
- patches/: the explored code (src/ only), in order, to apply on top of the
  commit above, i.e. on the parent of "slz: fix a mixed declaration and code
  in the text tree's loop". They add slz_rfc1951_encode_hist(),
  slz_rfc1951_pflush(), the SLZ_TAG_REFS option of the main encoder, and
  slz_rfc1951_encode_event() with all its build options (SLZ_EV_FMT,
  _HASH_BITS, _HIST, _MODE, _STEP...). The code each section measured:
    sections 3-7     0001 encode with a history and add a partial flush
    section 5b       0002 add an EOB-only flush
    section 10       0003 add an event encoder
    section 11       0004 allow 16-bit entries
    section 12       0005 tagged 4-byte entries, amortized history
    sections 13-15   0006 try 4-byte tagged entries in the main encoder
    section 16       0007 prefetch the reference table at entry
    section 17       0008 default to 2 kB of history
    section 18       0009 try a stack copy, a ring and a history-sized buffer
    section 19       0010-0011 in-place ring of the history's size (mode 4)
- ssecomp.c: the study's main tool, which needs these patches.
- evtest-study.c, evbench-study.c: the fuzzer and the concurrent-streams
  benchmark used for the measurements below, which take the context as an
  argument and need these patches as well.
- evtest.c, evbench.c: the same, ported to the integrated API (SLZ_CT_EVENT
  and the ring).

The question: AI and API gateways stream their responses as server-sent
events (SSE), each event repeating the same JSON envelope around a few bytes
of payload, with 50-100 ms between events. haproxy then has to flush the
compressor after each event, and slz compresses nothing that way, while the
whole capture compresses to 3%. How much history must the encoder keep
across calls to get most of it back (256, 512, 1 kB...)?

sse-response.txt is a real capture: 942 events, one per line. Each event is
sent as the line followed by "\n\n" (the SSE event terminator), 260568 bytes
in all, 277 bytes per event on average.


1. What the events contain
--------------------------

- The payload ("content" or "reasoning_content") is 4.4 bytes per event on
  average (4092 bytes in all), 1.6% of the data.
- Consecutive events share their first 71 bytes and their last 198 bytes on
  average. Only "created" (17 values over the capture) and "id" (2 values)
  change besides the payload.
So one previous event holds nearly every match an event needs.


2. Why slz compresses nothing today
-----------------------------------

The encoder resets its reference table at each call, so nothing can match
across calls: each event alone has no repetitions and goes out as a stored
block (101.7% of the input). The deflate format itself allows it: the
decoder keeps its 32 kB window across blocks and flushes.


3. The experiment
-----------------

- slz_rfc1951_encode_hist() (src/slz.c, EXP): like slz_rfc1951_encode(), but
  the <hlen> bytes before the input are history already sent, which is
  indexed into the reference table without being emitted. The encoder gets a
  <start> argument; the production _levX() functions pass 0 and compile to
  the same instructions (only padding moves).
- slz_rfc1951_pflush() (src/slz.c, EXP): a partial flush, see section 5.
- ssecomp.c: sends the events one call each, with up to N bytes of history,
  flushing after each one, then finishes. After every flush it checks with a
  streaming zlib inflater that everything up to the end of that event is
  decodable from the bytes emitted so far (the event can be delivered), and
  at the end that the whole stream decodes.
- zlib reference: ~/repo/claude-scripts/libslz/zlib_sse.py, with a fresh
  compressor per event primed with the last N bytes (zdict), which models a
  history of exactly N bytes, or one continuous stream with a 2^W window.

Build: gcc -O2 -Isrc experiments/events/ssecomp.c src/slz.c
src/slz_common.c -lz; run: ssecomp sse-response.txt <level> <hint> <hist>
[sync|partial|none] (hist -1: the whole capture in one call).


4. History size, slz level 1
----------------------------

Flush after each event (slz_rfc1951_flush(): EOB + empty stored block),
total output, ratio and bytes per event:

  history     sync flush
     0       265121  101.75%  281.4
    16       264027  101.33%  280.3
    32       237029   90.97%  251.6
    64       211399   81.13%  224.4
   128       188593   72.38%  200.2
   192        92111   35.35%   97.8
   256        34929   13.40%   37.1
   272        19936    7.65%   21.2
   288        13407    5.15%   14.2
   320-448    13353    5.12%   14.2
   512        14976    5.75%   15.9
   1024       15077    5.79%   16.0
   4096       14641    5.62%   15.5
   32768      14585    5.60%   15.5

  whole capture in one call:   8310  3.19%
  32 kB history, no flush:     9420  3.62%

The knee is at one event (288 bytes here, events being 277 bytes on
average): below, the long suffix match isn't reachable. More history doesn't
help, and 512 bytes and more even looks worse than 288-448, which isn't due
to the history: when a flush finds the output byte-aligned (no pending
bits), it emits nothing and the block stays open, which is free. The events
being nearly identical, their bit cost is nearly constant, and whether a run
of events stays aligned depends on the exact lengths of the matches. With
384 bytes, 419 of the 942 flushes found the output aligned; with 1024, the
encoder sometimes takes the suffix match two events back (<202,573> instead
of <201,286>), which changes the bit lengths and breaks the alignment more
often (317 events got 6 bytes larger, 27 got 6 bytes smaller).


5. The flush is the main cost
-----------------------------

A sync flush costs EOB (7 bits) + empty stored block header (3) + padding
(3.5 on average) + LEN/NLEN (32) + the next block's header (3), about 48
bits per event, for about 10 bytes of actual content. zlib's former
Z_PARTIAL_FLUSH does the job with EOB + an empty fixed block (10 bits),
emitting only the complete bytes: the 10 extra bits guarantee that the
closed block's EOB is within them, so the decoder can output everything,
and the rest of the empty block goes out with the next event. That's 20
bits per event with the next header. slz_rfc1951_pflush() does that (and
nothing when the output is aligned). Every event is still verified to be
decodable from the bytes emitted up to its flush:

  history     sync flush                partial flush
     0       265121 101.75% 281.4      265106 101.74% 281.4
   256        34929  13.40%  37.1       31016  11.90%  32.9
   288        13407   5.15%  14.2       11906   4.57%  12.6
   384        13353   5.12%  14.2       11852   4.55%  12.6
   512        14976   5.75%  15.9       11913   4.57%  12.6
   1024       15077   5.79%  16.0       11550   4.43%  12.3
   32768      14585   5.60%  15.5       11358   4.36%  12.1

With the partial flush the alignment luck matters much less, and the curve
is what one expects: a plateau from one event (4.57%), then a slow gain
(4.36% with 32 kB, 0.6 byte per event).

An alternative: not flushing at all, the last bits of an event leaving
with the next one (more per-event latency, same rate and same end of
response), the block staying open:

  history     no flush                  partial flush
   288        10285   3.95%  10.9       11906   4.57%  12.6
   512        10082   3.87%  10.7       11913   4.57%  12.6
   32768       9420   3.62%  10.0       11358   4.36%  12.1

The partial flush costs 1.6-2 bytes per event over not flushing at all,
the sync flush 3.3-5.5. The remaining gap to the whole capture in one call
(3.19%) is that matches can't span calls.

For comparison, zenc -1 -k 32 (32 kB calls, no history, no flush) gives
4.44% on the file (one "\n" per event, 259626 bytes) and -k 4096 3.20%: each
call starts without history, so the first event of each call is sent
nearly as literals. -k 4 gives 13.52%, -k 1 42.87%.


5b. An EOB-only flush, small hashes, and levels 2-4 in the fixed tree
---------------------------------------------------------------------

The partial flush's empty fixed block isn't needed to deliver the data: the
7 bits of EOB follow the last symbol and at most 7 bits stay queued, so the
last symbol is always entirely in the emitted bytes; the EOB itself may stay
partly queued. slz_rfc1951_eflush() (EXP) only sends EOB and the complete
bytes, the next call opening a new block: 10 bits per event with the next
header, instead of 20 for the partial flush and 48 for the sync one. Every
event is still verified delivered by zlib (ssecomp mode "eob"). Level 1:

  history     sync      partial    eob        no flush
   288        5.15%     4.57%      4.16%      3.95%
   512        5.75%     4.57%      4.14%      3.87%
   32768      5.60%     4.36%      4.00%      3.62%
  bytes/event at 512: 15.9 / 12.6 / 11.5 / 10.7

Hash size (SLZ_HASH_BITS, 8 bytes per entry), level 1, eob flush; the
history is re-indexed at each call, every position, which is a bit more than
a table kept between calls would hold (inside a match only its last 3
positions are inserted):

  bits  table     288      512      1024
   13   64 kB    4.16%    4.14%    4.06%
   12   32 kB    4.17%    4.11%    4.06%
   11   16 kB    4.25%    4.09%    4.07%
   10    8 kB    4.25%    4.14%    4.05%
    9    4 kB    4.26%    4.15%    4.08%
    8    2 kB    4.68%    4.56%    4.52%

With 512 bytes of history, 9 bits (4 kB) are as good as 13. 8 bits start to
lose matches.

Levels 2-4 with every block started in the fixed tree (ssecomp env FIXED=1,
which only sets cur_tree when a block is to be opened), eob flush:

  bits  history   L1       L2       L3       L4
   13    288     4.16%    4.17%    4.22%    4.25%
   13    512     4.14%    4.14%    4.17%    4.14%
   13    32768   4.00%    4.01%    4.00%    3.99%
    9    288     4.26%    4.27%    4.29%    4.17%
    9    512     4.15%    4.16%    4.16%    4.13%
    9    32768   3.96%    3.97%    3.96%    3.93%

The fixed tree removes the 29% problem at all levels, and the encoder never
switched to the text tree by itself. The level differences are within
0.1 pt: the backward extension of level 4 only shows with 288 bytes and a
9-bit hash (4.26% -> 4.17%), where it recovers match starts lost to
collisions.

6. zlib reference
-----------------

zlib, history of exactly N bytes (fresh compressor per event with the last
N bytes as dictionary), ratio:

                  -1                  -6                  -9
  history     sync   partial      sync   partial      sync   partial
     0       84.14%  82.69%      83.83%  82.38%      83.83%  82.38%
   256       13.28%  11.83%      13.20%  11.75%      13.20%  11.75%
   288        6.02%   4.58%       6.01%   4.56%       6.01%   4.56%
   512        5.96%   4.51%       5.94%   4.49%       5.94%   4.49%
   1024       5.87%   4.42%       5.76%   4.32%       5.76%   4.31%
   4096       5.67%   4.22%       5.46%   4.01%       5.43%   3.99%
   32768      5.70%   4.26%       5.34%   3.89%       5.25%   3.80%
  whole       2.66%               2.18%               2.04%

One continuous stream with a 512-byte window gives 81-83%: zlib keeps 262
bytes of lookahead out of its window, so only about 250 bytes can be
referenced, less than one event (1 kB: 4.61-6.61%, 32 kB: same as dict
32768 within 0.4 pt).

- One event of history is the knee for zlib too: with 288 bytes, -1, -6
  and -9 all give 4.56-4.58% with the partial flush, the same as slz
  (4.57%).
- Beyond one event, zlib -9 goes down to 3.80% with 32 kB (12.6 -> 10.5
  bytes per event), its better matcher finding older repeated tokens; slz
  to 4.36%.
- In both, the sync flush costs 3-4 bytes per event more than the partial
  one, about 1.3 points: the flush matters more than the history beyond
  one event.


7. Levels 2-4 and the text hint: the text tree's header at every event
---------------------------------------------------------------------

  ratio, partial flush       288 bytes    32 kB
  level 1                      4.57%      4.36%
  level 1 + text hint         29.01%     28.85%
  levels 2, 3, 4 (+/- hint)   29.01%     28.85%
  (sync flush, 32 kB: levels 2-4 28.86%)

From level 2 the stream starts with the text tree, each flush closes the
block, and each event opens a new one, paying the text tree's 78-byte
header every time (80 bytes per event). With so few literals per event, the
encoder never collects the evidence to go back to the fixed tree. The hint
makes level 1 work as level 3, so it's affected too, and haproxy would set
it for SSE since text/event-stream is a text type. Any stream flushed often
with little data per flush is concerned, history or not.


8. Conclusions
--------------

- A history of one event is enough to get most of the gain: about 300 bytes
  for this capture, so 512 bytes would cover events up to about 500 bytes.
  Beyond, slz gains 0.2 pt up to 32 kB, zlib -9 0.8 pt.
- With one event of history and a partial flush, slz level 1 gives 4.57%
  (12.6 bytes per event) against 101.7% today; the whole capture in one call
  gives 3.19%. The difference is the per-event flush and block overhead.
- The partial flush (EOB + empty fixed block, complete bytes only) saves
  1.5-3.5 bytes per event over the sync flush, with every event decodable
  from the bytes sent up to its flush.
- Levels 2-4 and the text hint are 6 times worse than level 1 on such
  streams (29%), because of the text tree's header at every event. They
  need to stay in the fixed tree when blocks are this small.


9. Not done yet / ideas
-----------------------

- The history has to survive between calls while the caller's buffer is
  gone, so the stream would need its own copy of the last N bytes (e.g. 512
  bytes per stream), re-indexed at each call (N inserts) or kept in a
  smaller reference table.
- Each call still resets the whole 64 kB reference table (memset), which is
  large next to a 280-byte event: a speed question, not measured here.
- When a flush finds the output aligned, keeping the block open is free;
  choosing between equivalent encodings to end aligned more often (e.g.
  splitting a match) could make more flushes free. Not tried.
- How the text tree should behave on flush-heavy streams (section 7): the
  fixed tree fixes it at all levels (section 5b).
- The 64 kB reset: the entries already hold the word and the hit test
  already checks the distance, so with positions counted from the start of
  the stream and the distance limit set to the history size, the entries
  from earlier calls are rejected by the existing test: no reset per call
  and no extra read. This needs the table kept in the stream, hence small
  (4 kB at 9 bits).


10. A dedicated event encoder: slz_rfc1951_encode_event()
---------------------------------------------------------

The design: a separate, small encoding function for streams of small
events (SSE, syslog), with the reference table and the history kept between
calls in a context passed by the caller, no reset, the fixed tree only, no
literal skip, and a backward match of 8 bytes (free in this context: here
the target is the ratio for reasonable resources, the 64 kB reset per call
being the cost it replaces).

- struct slz_ev_ctx (slz.h, EXP): the reference table, a 512-byte history
  and its fill length, allocated by the caller, initialized by
  slz_ev_init(). The hash size and the history size are its own build-time
  constants, separate from SLZ_HASH_BITS; slz.c alone knows the entry format.
- Positions are counted from the start of the stream (strm->ilen), so the
  entries from previous calls stay valid without any reset. A candidate must
  fall within the history or the input before the current position, and is
  verified by comparing the bytes themselves (the match starts at offset 0,
  4 bytes at least), which also rejects the entries aliased by the 32-bit
  wrapping (tested by starting the stream's position half the file below
  2^32, evtest WRAP=1).
- A source in the history may continue into the input: the comparison then
  continues at in[0]. The backward match stays within the source's segment.
  The last 3 positions of the history are indexed by the next call, once
  their 4th byte is known.
- Level 0 still sends stored blocks (haproxy lowers the level to 0 under
  CPU pressure) and keeps the history up to date.

Bug found on the way: a match reaching the end of the input (rem < 4) was not
indexed at all, which slz_rfc1951_encode() also does since it doesn't care
about the next call. Each event ends with the long match on the previous
event's tail, so when "created" changed, the entry point in the middle of the
tail (,"id":...) was never in the table: 6.39% instead of 4.14%. The
experiment of section 5b re-indexed the whole history at each call, which
hid it.

What to index after a match, eob flush, ratio:

  indexed positions        back 8    no back
  all of them              4.17%     4.52%
  1 in 4                   4.15%     4.34%
  1 in 16                  4.16%     4.28%
  1 in 32                  4.16%     4.29%
  1 in 64                  4.36%     4.49%
  first 3 (as slz.c)       5.12%     5.16%

With the backward match, one matched position in 16 or 32 is as good as all
of them. A step of 8 has a reason to work on any data: the first indexed
position after the point where the data diverge is at most 8 bytes away, and
the backward match recovers them. It's the default (SLZ_EV_STEP).

Cost, evbench: <n> streams, each with its own slz stream, context and
receive buffer, visited in a new random order at each round, one event each:
the event is copied into the stream's receive buffer (as recv() would), then
compressed and flushed. With 10000 streams the contexts total 25-46 MB, more
than the L3 cache (11 MB), so nothing of a stream is left in the caches when
it gets its next event. Xeon W-2145 (Skylake-X), gcc 13.3, -O2 with the JCC
erratum options, median of 7 interleaved runs, ns per event (the ratio at
10000 streams is higher because each stream only gets 100 events, the first
one without history):

                                          n=1     n=1000   n=10000
  today: encode level 1 + sync flush     1014     1034     1089    101.7%
  event, word kept, 4 kB, index all       299      469      795    4.10%
  event, word kept, 4 kB, index 1 in 8    161      283      645    4.11%
  event, no word, 4 kB, index 1 in 8      152      275      639    4.11%
  event, no word, 2 kB, index 1 in 8      165        -      562    4.11%
  event, no word, 1 kB, index 1 in 8      169        -      521    4.15%
  event, word kept, 4 kB, first 3         161      267      594    5.12%
  copy into the receive buffer only        15       25       54

(word kept: 8-byte entries with the matched word, 9 bits; no word: 4-byte
entries, 10, 9 or 8 bits.)

- Keeping or dropping the word makes little difference at the same table
  size: with the word, fewer candidates are compared, but the comparison is
  needed anyway on the real matches, which read the same history lines.
  With all positions indexed, the word is worth 2-9% (more candidates).
- Cold streams cost 3.5 times more than hot ones, and the table's size is
  what matters there: 4 kB -> 2 kB saves 12%, 1 kB another 7% for +0.04 pt.
  The default is no word, 9 bits (2 kB), step 8: 2.6 kB per stream with the
  history.
- Against what haproxy does today, the event encoder is 2-6 times cheaper per
  event (no 64 kB reset, no stored block) and gives 4.1% instead of 101.7%.

Reliability: evtest (random chunks from 1 byte, random sync/partial/EOB/no
flush, each flush checked as delivered by zlib, then the whole stream), on
the capture, random data, low-entropy binary and slz's sources, levels 0-4,
under ASan/UBSan, also across the 32-bit wrap: no failure.


11. 16-bit entries, and indexing 1 matched position in 4
--------------------------------------------------------

Since the table's size dominates the cost on cold streams, 16-bit entries
were tried. SLZ_EV_POS16 keeps only the 16 low bits of the position; the
lookup takes the most recent position with these bits, at most 65536 bytes
back (last = cur - (uint16_t)(cur - entry)). Older entries alias to it and
are rejected by the range check or by the comparison, as with the 32-bit
wrapping. Same matches, so the same ratios as 32-bit entries with the same
number of bits. evtest also passes with chunks up to 140 kB (aliasing within
a call), and across the 32-bit wrap.

Ratio on the capture, eob flush, the same with 16- and 32-bit entries:

  bits   step 1   step 2   step 4   step 8
   10    4.11%    4.15%    4.14%    4.14%
    9    4.17%    4.16%    4.15%    4.16%
    8    4.25%    4.17%    4.14%    4.17%

evbench, same conditions as section 10, ns per event:

                                n=1     n=1000   n=10000
  32-bit, 9 bits (2 kB), step 8   165      248      556
  16-bit, 10 bits (2 kB), step 8  157      241      548
  16-bit, 9 bits (1 kB), step 8   160      231      508
  16-bit, 8 bits (512 B), step 8  170      232      473    (+0.04 pt)
  16-bit, 10 bits, step 4         184      266      566
  16-bit, 9 bits, step 4          194      261      533
  16-bit, 8 bits, step 4          205      264      503    (+0.01 pt)

- At the same table size (2 kB), 16-bit entries only gain 1.5%: it's the
  size that matters. Halving it to 1 kB saves 7% on cold streams, 512 bytes
  another 7% for +0.04 pt.
- Step 4 costs 25-35 ns per event more than step 8 and gains at most 0.01
  pt on this capture, except with 8 bits where it compensates the collisions
  (4.14% instead of 4.17%, but still 30 ns more). On SSE, step 8 is enough;
  step 4 might pay on less repetitive data such as syslog, not measured
  here for lack of a sample.
- New default: 16-bit entries, 9 bits (1 kB), step 8, 1.5 kB per stream
  with the history.


12. Logs: history size, step, entry format
------------------------------------------

Production log samples were used, not in the tree: haproxy logs,
maillog, messages and netfilter logs, 100k lines each (96k for messages),
361, 135, 104 and 224 bytes per line on average. Each line is one event
(ssecomp LF=1), compressed with slz_rfc1951_encode_event() and the eob flush
unless stated otherwise.

Ratio against the history size, step 8, with the hash size that suits it
(9 bits at 512, 11 at 4 kB, 13 above):

  history    haproxy   maillog   messages   netfilter
    512      50.53%    37.32%    27.85%     27.90%
   4096      31.58%    18.38%    20.43%     17.95%
  16384      28.03%    15.32%    17.88%     15.12%
  32768      27.03%    14.94%    17.27%     14.53%
  whole file, one call, slz level 1:
             26.63%    14.23%    15.75%     13.82%
  whole file, one call, slz level 3:
             24.08%    13.20%    14.39%     12.83%

Unlike SSE, where one event of history is enough, logs keep gaining up to
32 kB: from 512 bytes to 4 kB the output is divided by 1.4 to 2, and with
32 kB it's within 0.4-1.5 pt of the whole file compressed in one call.

The full grid (history 512 to 32768, 9 to 13 bits, steps 1, 4, 8) shows:
- The hash must grow with the history: about 1 entry per 2-4 bytes of
  history. With too small a table, step 8 is better than step 1 (fewer
  collisions), e.g. haproxy at 32 kB with 9 bits: 33.14% at step 8, 35.11%
  at step 1.
- With a suitable table, the step changes at most 0.3 pt: step 4 is the
  best for haproxy (31.42% at 4 kB/11 bits against 31.58% at step 8), step 1
  for messages and netfilter (20.13% and 17.71% against 20.43% and 17.95%),
  all equal for maillog. Step 4 is thus a reasonable compromise, but not a
  large gain.

Backward match, 4 kB and 16 kB, step 8: without it, the output grows by
0.9 pt / 0.4 pt (haproxy), 0.6 / 0.2 (maillog), 0.4 / 0.15 (messages),
0.4 / 0.14 (netfilter).

Flushes, 4 kB / 11 bits, ratio with eob, partial, sync, no flush:

  haproxy     31.58%   31.88%   32.73%   31.27%
  maillog     18.38%   19.21%   21.50%   17.58%
  messages    20.43%   21.49%   24.18%   19.38%
  netfilter   17.95%   18.44%   19.83%   17.47%

zlib with one stream and a flush per line (zlib_sse.py LF=1), window 4 kB /
32 kB, partial flush:

               -1                -6                -9
  haproxy    31.93% 26.76%    26.70% 22.37%    26.59% 21.90%
  maillog    20.36% 15.95%    16.86% 13.19%    16.69% 12.84%
  messages   22.13% 18.36%    19.29% 15.80%    19.16% 15.45%
  netfilter  20.60% 15.40%    15.94% 12.54%    15.60% 11.84%

With the same history, the event encoder is better than zlib -1 flushed per
line on all four but haproxy at 32 kB (27.03% vs 26.76%), thanks to the
cheaper flush and the backward match; zlib -6 stays 2-5 pt below.

Entry formats (SLZ_EV_FMT): 8 = 32-bit position + word, 4 = 16-bit position
+ 16-bit tag of the word (new), 2 = 16-bit position. Same ratios (the
candidates they reject would fail the comparison anyway). evbench LF=1, ns
per event:

                     hot (1 stream)             cold (1000 streams)
  history          8       4       2           8       4       2
  haproxy  512   1177    1252    2255        1513    1535    2483
          4096    846     891    1262        2587    1950    1986
         16384    881     889    1079        3875    3610    3521
         32768    834     842     983        3851    3608    3804
  maillog  512    392     409     547         628     602     707
          4096    255     266     279         901     746     655
         16384    217     225     226        1070    1006     939
         32768    213     221     227        1071     995     968

(the 1000-stream column used 64 kB receive buffers per stream, the others
in this section are sized to the longest event; the SSE figures below are
all with the latter.)

- On literal-heavy data (haproxy logs at 512 bytes: 50%), 2-byte entries
  are twice as slow: every lookup finds an in-range candidate that the
  comparison has to reject. The word or the tag avoids most of them.
- On cold streams, the table size matters again, and the 4-byte tag format
  is never far from the best one. It's the default now.
- Cold streams with 16-32 kB of history cost 3.6 µs per haproxy log line,
  1 µs per mail log line: the history's lines are cold too.

SSE, 512 bytes, same evbench, 1 and 10000 streams: previous version (single
512-byte history, 2-byte entries) 160 / 480 ns, now with 2-byte entries
160 / 509, 4-byte tags 174 / 587, 8-byte entries 163 / 653.

The history used to be shifted by memmove() at each call, which costs up to
250 ns per event at 32 kB (maillog: 313 ns per event at 16 kB, 558 at 32
kB). It's now in a buffer twice as large which only slides when full, so
that one byte is copied per input byte on average. This costs 6% on cold
SSE streams at 512 bytes (480 -> 509 ns), where the memmove was cheap.

So the right settings differ: SSE wants a small table and 512 bytes of
history, logs want 4-32 kB and a table that grows with it. Since the
context's size follows from them, the history size and the hash size would
better be chosen per stream at run time (the history size is already a
variable in the loop, the hash size would be a variable shift).


13. SSE with 4 kB of history
----------------------------

4 kB is considered the maximum history for the event encoder (memory per
stream). On the SSE capture, eob flush, ratio (2- and 4-byte entries give
the same):

  history   bits   ratio
    512       9    4.16%
   4096       9    4.00%
   4096      10    3.99%
   4096      11    3.97%

evbench (in this run 4.11% at 512 and 3.90-3.93% at 4 kB, the events being
taken in a different order), median of 5, ns per event:

                              n=1    n=1000   n=10000
  2-byte, 512 B, 9 bits       165      234      511
  2-byte, 4 kB, 9 bits        167      311      689
  2-byte, 4 kB, 11 bits       151      374      784
  4-byte, 512 B, 9 bits       174      258      590
  4-byte, 4 kB, 9 bits        165      317      708
  4-byte, 4 kB, 11 bits       168      488      915

4 kB saves 4-5% of the output on SSE (0.2 pt), the same cost on hot streams,
and 35-55% more on cold ones, mostly from the larger table: with the table
kept at 9 bits, the larger history alone costs 35% (511 -> 689 ns) for most
of the gain (4.00% against 3.97%).


14. The tag in the main encoder (SLZ_TAG_REFS)
----------------------------------------------

Same 4-byte format as the event encoder's default, applied to
_slz_rfc1951_encode() under SLZ_TAG_REFS=1 (off by default, the default
build's _lev1-4 are unchanged, checked with fncmp): 16 low bits of the
position + 16-bit tag (w ^ w >> 16), the position rebuilt as the most recent
one with these bits, rejected if before the buffer, and verified by starting
memmatch at offset 0 (at least 4 bytes). The probes of levels 3-4 compare the
tag. Round trips at levels 1-4 on silesia and linux-7.0 and streamtest pass.

Ratio: with 13 bits (32 kB table instead of 64), identical to today. With 14
bits (64 kB, as today), silesia 46.28 / 43.73 / 40.52 / 40.12% at -1..-4
instead of 46.61 / 43.98 / 40.84 / 40.47%, linux 23.27 / 20.76 / 20.58 /
20.58% instead of 23.36 / 20.80 / 20.63 / 20.63%.

Speed, zenc -p -t -v, Xeon W-2145, gcc 13.3 -O3 -march=native -mno-avx512f
with the JCC erratum options, 4 .text paddings x best of 3, mean (min-max)
over paddings (zsweep.sh in claude-scripts):

                    today            tag 13 bits        tag 14 bits
  silesia -1   589.3 (589-590)   522.0 (521-524)    506.7 (506-507)
  silesia -4   459.7 (458-460)   418.7 (417-420)    403.3 (402-405)
  linux -1     687.2 (687-688)   594.9 (594-595)    576.6 (575-578)
  linux -4     642.5 (640-644)   570.9 (570-572)    556.0 (555-557)

The tag costs 9-13% at 13 bits and 12-16% at 14 bits, with no overlap
between the paddings. perf stat, silesia -1, padding 0: +14% instructions,
+11% cycles, +16% branch misses. Every position pays the tag computation
and the position rebuild, and the hit path gets two more tests (rebuilt
position before the buffer, fewer than 4 matching bytes). The smaller table
doesn't pay for it: today's 64 kB table is already hot in L2 within a call.
The event encoder is different: its table is cold at each call on busy
proxies, and it already compared the bytes from offset 0.

This confirms an earlier negative result on the main encoder.


15. 1 kB of history, steps 1 to 8, 9 bits
-----------------------------------------

It was suspected that 1 kB (about 3 SSE events, so more chances of a long
match) with one matched position in 2 indexed and 9 bits could be a good
point. Note that the step only concerns the positions inside a match:
every literal position is still looked up and indexed.

Ratio, eob flush, step 1 / 2 / 4 / 8:

  9 bits
  history  SSE                        haproxy                    maillog
    512    4.17 4.16 4.15 4.16        49.83 49.40 49.68 50.53    36.71 36.84 37.21 37.32
