- 1 kB instead of 512 bytes: SSE 4.16 -> 4.05% (step 8), logs 12-18%
  smaller. 4 kB remains much better on logs (maillog 31.4 -> 19.5%).
- At 1 kB / 9 bits, step 2 is better than step 8 by 0.3-0.9 pt on the logs
  (haproxy 40.73 against 41.59%, messages 23.98 against 24.57%), equal on SSE.
  At 4 kB / 9 bits it's no longer better on haproxy, maillog and netfilter:
  the table is too small for that many entries.

Cost, evbench, 9 bits, ns per event (SSE median of 5, logs median of 3):

                        SSE                  haproxy          maillog
  fmt hist  step   n=1  n=1000 n=10000    n=1   n=1000    n=1   n=1000
   2   512    8    161    233    508     2252    2480     538     696
   2  1024    2    233    309    616     1941    2167     521     703
   2  1024    8    163    246    562     1868    2115     494     683
   2  4096    2    230    360    723     1583    1974     363     626
   2  4096    8    164    312    693     1401    1818     331     608
   4   512    8    174    258    587     1251    1514     416     597
   4  1024    2    261    348    694     1104    1368     390     571
   4  1024    8    171    266    637     1050    1320     371     585
   4  4096    2    248    383    772      977    1353     307     563
   4  4096    8    165    317    714      869    1279     269     546

(ratios in this SSE run: 4.11% at 512, 3.98-4.00% at 1 kB, 3.91-3.93% at
4 kB.)

- On logs, a longer history is cheaper as well as smaller, there are
  fewer literals to look up: 4 kB / step 8 / 4-byte tags costs 869 ns per
  haproxy line against 1251 at 512 bytes, and 15-36% less cold.
- Step 2 costs 40-90 ns per event hot (SSE +70, haproxy +55-110), less cold,
  for 0.3-0.9 pt on logs at 1 kB and nothing on SSE.
- The 4-byte tags win on logs (30-45% cheaper on haproxy), the 2-byte
  entries on SSE (8-13% cheaper): literal-heavy data needs the filter.


16. 2 kB of history, and prefetching the context at entry
---------------------------------------------------------

2 kB, 9 bits (the 4-byte table is 2 kB too), evbench, ns per event,
median of 5 (SSE) or 3 (logs), the same run for each column:

                           SSE                  haproxy          maillog
  fmt hist  step    n=1  n=1000 n=10000     n=1   n=1000     n=1   n=1000
   4  1024    8     172    269    638      1050    1323      370    572
   4  2048    4     200    308    700       959    1279      317    544
   4  2048    8     169    286    685       928    1260      304    536
   4  4096    8     168    320    717       865    1287      270    545
   2  1024    8     162    247    561      1872    2115      498    683
   2  2048    4     197    299    653      1614    1876      404    627
   2  2048    8     166    273    632      1565    1843      394    623
   2  4096    8     166    318    693      1395    1845      331    603

ratio (n=1)        SSE      haproxy   maillog
  1024 / step 8   3.98%     41.59%    31.41%
  2048 / step 4   3.97%     36.31%    23.58%
  2048 / step 8   3.98%     36.71%    23.49%
  4096 / step 8   3.93%     34.20%    19.52%

2 kB costs 7% more than 1 kB on cold SSE streams for the same ratio there,
and is 10-25% smaller and 2-18% cheaper on logs. Step 4 isn't worth it.

Prefetch at entry (SLZ_EV_PREFETCH): 1 = every line of the
table, 2 = the table and the history. 9 bits, step 8:

  SSE              n=1          n=1000          n=10000
  fmt hist      0   1   2     0   1   2       0   1   2
   2  1024    162 159 166   246 240 251     562 494 465
   2  2048    164 163 171   270 257 301     633 561 561
   4  1024    171 172 174   265 272 288     634 567 532
   4  2048    167 168 172   282 285 334     682 614 624

  haproxy          n=1             n=1000             n=10000
   2  1024   1872 1870 1893   2122 2064 2077   2580 2374 2276
   2  2048   1564 1571 1591   1862 1802 1797   2523 2309 2077
   4  1024   1048 1065 1061   1328 1278 1260   1903 1579 1513
   4  2048    924  926  941   1246 1192 1175   1903 1592 1483

  maillog          n=1             n=1000             n=10000
   2  1024    500  495  502    685  647  634   1083  912  819
   2  2048    393  393  401    621  579  572   1058  876  777
   4  1024    370  373  377    574  530  512   1028  793  725
   4  2048    303  304  314    534  496  491   1015  785  730

(at n=10000 each log stream only gets 20 lines, hence the higher ratios.)

- Prefetching the table saves 10-12% on cold SSE streams, 8-23% on cold log
  streams, 3-8% at 1000 streams on logs, and is neutral on hot streams.
- Prefetching the history as well saves another 5-11% on cold logs and on
  SSE at 1 kB, but costs 5-18% on SSE at 1000 streams (contexts in L3) and
  nothing at 2 kB cold: SSE only references the last one or two events, so
  most of a 2 kB history is prefetched for nothing. Prefetching only the
  tail of the history is left to try.
- Default now SLZ_EV_PREFETCH=1.


17. Default settings
--------------------

The choice: 2 kB of history with the 4-byte tagged entries on 9 bits
(2 kB table), step 8, backward match 8, table prefetched at entry. It excels
nowhere but is bad nowhere either: SSE 4.05%, haproxy 36.71%, maillog
23.49%, messages 22.16%, netfilter 21.47% (eob flush), 6 kB per stream
(2 kB table + 4 kB history buffer).

On the history prefetch: a reference is only read after the
table has eliminated most candidates, and the CPU starts fetching it a few
instructions after loading the entry anyway. The measured 5-11% on cold
log streams likely comes from issuing all the history's lines at once
instead of one dependent miss after the other (hash -> entry -> history),
which only pays when many of them get used, as on logs.

(Correction: section 17 first said 4.5 kB per stream; 2 kB of table + a
4 kB history buffer is 6 kB, and so was the commit message of patch 0008,
"event encoder: default to 2 kB of history".)


18. Where the history lives (SLZ_EV_MODE)
-----------------------------------------

The history buffer twice the history's size was questioned, with the
suggestion of copying the history to the stack with the input behind it (contiguous, and
hot), or else a buffer of the history's size whose tail is moved on exit.
Four modes, same matches (same ratios), all fuzzed:

  0: matched in place, history then input; buffer twice as large, sliding
     when full (until now)
  1: history copied to the stack with the input behind it, one contiguous
     buffer; buffer of the history's size, tail moved on exit; inputs above
     2 kB matched in place as 0
  2: matched in place as 0; buffer of the history's size, tail moved on exit
  3: as 1, but the buffer is a ring: straightened by two memcpy() when
     copied to the stack, only the input appended on exit; larger inputs
     are encoded in 2 kB slices, so there is no in-place path at all

2 kB of history, 4-byte tags, 9 bits, step 8, table prefetch, ns per event
(SSE median of 5, logs median of 3):

             SSE                     haproxy                  maillog
  mode   n=1  n=1000 n=10000    n=1  n=1000 n=10000     n=1  n=1000 n=10000
   0     168    285    617      927   1194   1589       307    496    784
   1     192    335    642      915   1137   1470       323    488    744
   2     181    309    668      944   1185   1583       321    500    788
   3     194    323    651      921   1123   1475       318    472    749

  per stream: mode 0 6 kB, modes 1-3 4 kB.

- Mode 2 (history-sized buffer, tail moved on exit) costs 7-9% on SSE,
  nothing on logs, and saves 2 kB per stream.
- Copying the history to the stack (1, 3) gains 5-8% on cold log streams
  and 6% at 1000 streams for haproxy and maillog with the ring (the copy
  acts as the history prefetch of section 16), but costs 14-15% on hot SSE
  streams and 4-6% on cold ones: 2 kB copied for a 280-byte event, of which
  SSE only uses the last one or two events.
- The ring (3) saves the tail move of 1 and gives the simplest encoder (no
  in-place matching, no special case at the history's end), for the same
  cost as 1.
No mode wins everywhere: 0 for SSE, 3 for logs. 2 trades 7-9% on SSE for 2
kB per stream. The default stays 0 for now.


19. A ring matched in place (mode 4)
------------------------------------

The next suggestion: a ring of the history's size, 64-byte aligned, used in
the split mode and only appended to on exit. Extra slack only helps the
"slide when full" scheme, and only when it is at least as large as the
events (with S bytes of slack, HIST/S bytes are moved per input byte), so
the ring has none: nothing is overwritten during the compression anyway.
When it wraps, the history is in two parts, so a match's source may run
from the old part to the wrapped part, then into the input; only such
matches pay for the third segment. The backward match stays within the
source's physical segment. 4 kB per stream (2 kB table + 2 kB ring). Same
ratios (3.97 instead of 3.98% on SSE in evbench, the backward match being
limited differently), fuzzed.

Same conditions as section 18, all four rebuilt with the 64-byte aligned
context, ns per event:

             SSE                     haproxy                  maillog
  mode   n=1  n=1000 n=10000    n=1  n=1000 n=10000     n=1  n=1000 n=10000
   0     168    291    612      914   1183   1589       303    499    810
   2     179    310    671      919   1166   1581       313    495    810
   3     198    327    652      919   1133   1479       319    480    762
   4     177    272    551      971   1204   1564       321    493    780

- Against mode 0, the in-place ring is 6% slower on hot streams (single
  build each, so possibly partly layout), but 6% faster on SSE at 1000
  streams and 10% faster at 10000, and within -4% to +2% on cold logs.
- It's the fastest of all modes on cold SSE streams, and saves 2 kB per
  stream over mode 0.
- On cold logs, the stack copy (3) remains the fastest (-5 to -7%).

Default now SLZ_EV_MODE=4: 2 kB ring matched in place, 2 kB table
of 4-byte tagged entries on 9 bits, step 8, backward match 8, table
prefetched at entry, 4 kB per stream.


20. Integration
---------------

A first integration was mistakenly based on an older code base, without the
automatic tree; the final one is on top of the automatic tree, where
send_eob() and the copy_lit helpers take the tree, and the open block's tree is <cur_tree>: the flush closes the block
with send_eob(strm, strm->cur_tree), and the event encoder first closes a
block opened with the text tree and resets <cur_tree> to the fixed one.
<content_type> grows to 2 bits, which fit in the padding before <debt>
(struct slz_stream: 56 bytes with the ring pointer, 48 before).

The integration was meant to be minimal: a cheaper flush, the event encoder
selected by a new SLZ_CT_EVENT content type, an exported size for the
caller to allocate the ring, and a check that the ring is set. The encoder
is the default above, alone, with its settings hardcoded: mode 4, 4-byte
tagged entries, 9 bits, 2 kB, step 8, backward match 8, table prefetch.

- The ring is private to slz.c (struct slz_ring: table, history, write
  offset, length, end position), slz_ring_size gives its size (4108
  bytes). The caller allocates it zeroed and sets it in the stream's new <ring> field, which
  the init functions reset. 64-byte alignment is preferred, not required:
  the table comes first, so the history is aligned when the ring is.
- slz_rfc1951_encode() uses the event encoder when the content type is
  SLZ_CT_EVENT, the ring is set and the level is not 0. Otherwise the
  stream works as with SLZ_CT_GENERIC (every tree selection in slz.c tests
  for SLZ_CT_TEXT, so 2 gets the fixed tree). The gzip and zlib encoders
  call it.
- New: the ring records the stream position where its history ends, and
  the history is dropped when it differs from strm->ilen, i.e. when data
  were passed without the ring (unset for a while, level 0, another
  content type). Without it, the distances would point to the wrong bytes.
- The flush: haproxy only needs every byte of data on the wire after a
  flush (http-no-delay, CouchDB's heartbeats), and slz_rfc1951_flush()
  already left the block open when entered with an empty queue. Its empty
  stored block only served to align the output, and dates back to when the
  EOB state implied an empty queue, as after a stored block. So instead of
  a separate EOB-only flush, slz_rfc1951_flush() itself now guarantees
  that all the bits pending at the call are emitted, adding only what is
  needed to cross the byte boundary (no symbol unless needed), from
  the state alone: nothing if aligned, the EOB inside a block (7 bits
  fixed, 11 text, checked at build time), EOB + padding in the last block,
  padding after it, an empty fixed block (10 bits) between blocks. Then
  only complete bytes are emitted, so up to 7 of the added bits may stay
  queued. A first version relied on the queue being empty between blocks
  except after a flush, which was rejected as depending on relations
  between states that nobody can check by reading the function. It
  applies to all content types: 0 or 10 bits (EOB + next header) instead
  of about 48 for a flush inside a block, 10 bits for a flush following
  another one with unaligned bits left. Sending a second
  EOB (empty fixed block, zlib's Z_PARTIAL_FLUSH) to get the first one out
  as well isn't needed by zlib, and Go's flate (read from its source, not
  run) only hands data to the reader when its window fills or at the end
  of the final block, so a block boundary doesn't help it either.
  Measured on SSE at level 1 with 512 B of history (section 5b): 15.9
  bytes per event with the old flush, 12.6 with Z_PARTIAL_FLUSH's, 11.5
  with this one, 10.7 without flushing. The plain level 1 path barely
  changes on SSE (265100 bytes instead of 265119 in evbench "today"):
  without history each event leaves as a stored block, already aligned.
- The backward match reads 8 bytes at once when UNALIGNED_LE_OK and 8 bytes
  are available before both sides, otherwise compares byte by byte.
- SLZ_DIRECT_ENQUEUE56 was first left out of the event encoder without
  being measured, which was noticed during the review. It is now supported like in the main
  encoder. Same output; evbench, 7 interleaved runs pinned on one core,
  median ns per event (min-max), Xeon W-2145, gcc 13.3 -O3:

                        without              with              delta
    SSE      n=1     182.8 (179.7-260.4)  179.6 (179.3-185.0)   -1.8%
    SSE      n=10000 596.4 (589.5-643.1)  591.0 (589.7-603.8)   -0.9%
    haproxy  n=1     981.7 (979.7-1169.8) 982.8 (978.8-997.2)   +0.1%
    haproxy  n=10000 1542.2 (1539.8-1560.1) 1546.4 (1545.0-1550.6) +0.3%

  Within the noise on SSE (the ranges overlap), +0.3% on the haproxy log
  at n=10000 (no overlap but tiny). Kept for consistency with the main
  encoder, since the option then means the same thing everywhere.

Checks:
- zenc's output is identical to the base's on silesia at levels 0-4,
  with and without -T.
- evtest (ASan/UBSan, alignment excepted): sse-response.txt, the first
  400 kB of silesia and the last 300 kB of the haproxy log, random levels,
  formats and content types (event, generic, text), one or two flushes in
  a row or none, delivery checked with zlib after each flush, 60 runs
  each, plus WRAP=1 (20 runs, chunks up to 140 kB) and DETACH=1 (ring
  removed for 1 chunk in 16): 0 failures.
- tests/sequence: 300000 sequences, 0 mismatches, 0 bound violations with
  the new flush bounds (6/8/16 bytes for deflate/zlib/gzip). Its total
  budget for a stream didn't count SLZ_MAX_DEBT_BYTES, which the text tree
  uses from level 2 since the automatic tree (81 streams over by up to 13
  bytes on the base, 88 with the new flush and its smaller budget,
  77 with the old budget). This is fixed by "tests/sequence: check each
  call against SLZ_MAX_OVERHEAD and allow the debt", which also checks each encode() call against SLZ_MAX_OVERHEAD().
- evbench outputs exactly the same number of bytes as the study's encoder
  (patch 0011, mode 4) with its EOB-only flush on SSE and logs.

evbench, study (patch 0011, mode 4) vs the integrated encoder (with the EOB-only
flush, before it was merged into slz_rfc1951_flush(), same output), 7
interleaved runs, median ns per event, Xeon W-2145, gcc 13.3:

                       n=1     n=1000   n=10000   ratio
  SSE         study   185.3    282.8     557.3    3.97%
              int     179.6    275.7     546.4
  haproxy     study  1107.3   1337.6    1699.7   36.71%
              int     989.0   1215.3    1556.4
  maillog     study   348.5      -       833.1   23.49%
              int     323.3      -       795.5

These figures are from the first integration (on the older code base). The
final one runs at the same speed (5 interleaved runs: SSE
183.1/551.8 ns at n=1/10000 vs 183.5/553.4, haproxy log 1034.9/1606.1 vs
1049.8/1627.4), so the 2-3% gained on SSE and 5-11% on logs over the study's
encoder don't come from the helpers, but from the encoder itself (the
study's version carries all the modes and options). Not investigated further. Same ratios. At n=1, SSE gives 3.97%
instead of ssecomp's 4.05% because these runs go through the capture about
1000 times; one pass (942 rounds) gives 4.05% with both.
